1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,778
A friend says the shop is
about one kilometre away.

2
00:00:03,778 --> 00:00:06,146
A map says nine sixty three metres.

3
00:00:06,546 --> 00:00:07,573
Both are true.

4
00:00:07,573 --> 00:00:11,316
But one tells you far more about
how carefully it was measured.

5
00:00:11,766 --> 00:00:13,695
Diya sees it in her kitchen.

6
00:00:13,695 --> 00:00:16,804
The kitchen scale shows
two fifty grams of flour.

7
00:00:17,154 --> 00:00:20,459
The lab balance: two
fifty point zero grams.

8
00:00:20,459 --> 00:00:21,463
Same flour.

9
00:00:21,963 --> 00:00:24,324
Is that extra zero just decoration?

10
00:00:24,974 --> 00:00:28,551
Every measurement has digits we
are sure of, and one we guessed.

11
00:00:28,901 --> 00:00:31,618
The sure digits are
called reliable digits.

12
00:00:31,618 --> 00:00:34,441
The last guessed one
is the uncertain digit.

13
00:00:34,791 --> 00:00:39,102
The reliable digits plus the first
uncertain digit are the significant

14
00:00:39,102 --> 00:00:40,002
figures.

15
00:00:40,479 --> 00:00:43,119
Let's measure a pencil
on our measuring bench.

16
00:00:43,469 --> 00:00:46,261
First, a ruler marked only in centimetres.

17
00:00:46,261 --> 00:00:49,412
The tip is past seven,
a little before halfway.

18
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:52,559
So we write seven point four centimetres.

19
00:00:52,559 --> 00:00:53,700
Seven is sure.

20
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:55,104
Four is our guess.

21
00:00:55,104 --> 00:00:56,750
Two significant figures.

22
00:00:57,150 --> 00:00:59,397
Now a ruler with millimetre marks.

23
00:00:59,397 --> 00:01:01,653
The tip is past seven point four.

24
00:01:01,653 --> 00:01:04,853
We guess one more digit:
seven point four three.

25
00:01:05,253 --> 00:01:07,002
Three significant figures.

26
00:01:07,002 --> 00:01:09,761
A finer scale gives one more sure digit.

27
00:01:10,161 --> 00:01:13,864
Each ten times finer scale
adds one significant figure.

28
00:01:13,864 --> 00:01:16,583
More significant figures, more precise.

29
00:01:17,233 --> 00:01:19,604
Let's replay the reading in slow motion.

30
00:01:19,954 --> 00:01:21,997
The tip stops between two marks.

31
00:01:21,997 --> 00:01:23,911
Up to the last mark is sure.

32
00:01:23,911 --> 00:01:25,390
The gap is the guess.

33
00:01:25,990 --> 00:01:29,243
So how do we count significant
figures in a number?

34
00:01:29,243 --> 00:01:31,167
Four rules, one at a time.

35
00:01:31,517 --> 00:01:32,439
Rule one.

36
00:01:32,439 --> 00:01:35,024
Every non-zero digit is significant.

37
00:01:35,024 --> 00:01:37,048
Two four three has three.

38
00:01:37,798 --> 00:01:38,773
Rule two.

39
00:01:38,773 --> 00:01:42,245
A zero between non-zero
digits is significant.

40
00:01:42,245 --> 00:01:44,326
Two zero five has three.

41
00:01:45,076 --> 00:01:46,149
Rule three.

42
00:01:46,149 --> 00:01:49,985
Zeros before the first
non-zero digit do not count.

43
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:53,413
Zero point zero zero
two five has only two.

44
00:01:54,163 --> 00:01:55,063
Rule four.

45
00:01:55,047 --> 00:01:58,400
Zeros at the end count
only with a decimal point.

46
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:00,544
Two fifty point zero has four.

47
00:02:00,544 --> 00:02:03,156
Two fifty, with no point, has two.

48
00:02:03,906 --> 00:02:05,691
Why don't front zeros count?

49
00:02:05,691 --> 00:02:06,894
Follow the links.

50
00:02:07,244 --> 00:02:10,269
Take two point three
zero eight centimetres.

51
00:02:10,269 --> 00:02:12,040
Four significant figures.

52
00:02:12,440 --> 00:02:15,876
In millimetres, it is twenty
three point zero eight.

53
00:02:15,876 --> 00:02:19,115
In metres, zero point
zero two three zero eight.

54
00:02:19,515 --> 00:02:22,118
The pencil and the ruler did not change.

55
00:02:22,118 --> 00:02:24,120
So the precision cannot change.

56
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:27,500
The front zeros came only
from choosing metres.

57
00:02:27,500 --> 00:02:29,735
They just place the decimal point.

58
00:02:30,385 --> 00:02:31,517
Remember this.

59
00:02:31,517 --> 00:02:33,887
Significant figures show precision.

60
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:36,490
Changing the unit never changes them.

61
00:02:37,190 --> 00:02:38,305
Side by side.

62
00:02:38,305 --> 00:02:41,113
The kitchen scale writes two fifty grams.

63
00:02:41,113 --> 00:02:44,406
No decimal point, so the
rule counts only two.

64
00:02:44,406 --> 00:02:46,099
Was that zero measured?

65
00:02:46,099 --> 00:02:47,317
We cannot tell.

66
00:02:47,717 --> 00:02:50,650
The lab balance writes
two fifty point zero.

67
00:02:50,650 --> 00:02:53,783
The decimal point shows
every zero was measured.

68
00:02:53,783 --> 00:02:55,465
Four significant figures.

69
00:02:56,065 --> 00:02:57,049
Your turn.

70
00:02:57,049 --> 00:03:01,934
How many significant figures in
zero point zero zero two five zero?

71
00:03:01,934 --> 00:03:03,325
Two, or three?

72
00:03:06,525 --> 00:03:07,425
Three!

73
00:03:07,138 --> 00:03:09,668
The front zeros only place the point.

74
00:03:09,668 --> 00:03:13,494
The last zero comes after a
decimal point, so it counts.

75
00:03:14,144 --> 00:03:18,719
Multiply or divide: keep as many
significant figures as the measurement

76
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:20,009
with the fewest.

77
00:03:20,409 --> 00:03:25,018
Add or subtract: keep as many decimal
places as the measurement with the

78
00:03:25,018 --> 00:03:25,918
fewest.

79
00:03:26,290 --> 00:03:27,190
Why?

80
00:03:26,798 --> 00:03:30,680
An answer is never more precise
than its weakest measurement.

81
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:31,980
Try this.

82
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:36,053
Two point zero times three point
one four is six point two eight.

83
00:03:36,053 --> 00:03:39,573
Do we write six point two
eight, or six point three?

84
00:03:42,773 --> 00:03:44,070
Six point three!

85
00:03:44,070 --> 00:03:49,111
Two point zero has only two significant
figures, so the answer keeps two.

86
00:03:49,761 --> 00:03:51,496
Now, one worked example.

87
00:03:51,846 --> 00:03:56,880
A metal block: mass thirty nine point
six grams, volume five point zero

88
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:58,275
cubic centimetres.

89
00:03:58,275 --> 00:03:59,682
Find its density.

90
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:02,430
Density is mass over volume.

91
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:05,750
Thirty nine point six
over five point zero.

92
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:09,169
The calculator says seven point nine two.

93
00:04:09,919 --> 00:04:13,554
Thirty nine point six has
three significant figures.

94
00:04:13,554 --> 00:04:15,464
Five point zero has two.

95
00:04:15,464 --> 00:04:16,364
Keep two.

96
00:04:17,109 --> 00:04:21,155
So the density is seven point
nine grams per cubic centimetre.

97
00:04:21,805 --> 00:04:23,271
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:23,621 --> 00:04:26,904
Many students use the
multiply rule when adding.

99
00:04:26,904 --> 00:04:31,875
Add one fifty two point three six,
twenty point four, and zero point five

100
00:04:31,875 --> 00:04:32,775
one two.

101
00:04:33,033 --> 00:04:36,300
The sum is one seventy
three point two seven two.

102
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:38,181
One seventy three is wrong.

103
00:04:38,181 --> 00:04:43,111
Twenty point four has one decimal place,
so: one seventy three point three.

104
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:45,258
Where is this asked?

105
00:04:45,258 --> 00:04:48,906
Count significant figures,
or round a calculated answer.

106
00:04:49,506 --> 00:04:50,772
Exam level now.

107
00:04:50,772 --> 00:04:55,956
A card is twelve point six zero centimetres
by four point three centimetres.

108
00:04:55,956 --> 00:04:59,477
What is its area, to
correct significant figures?

109
00:04:59,477 --> 00:05:00,797
Pause and solve.

110
00:05:06,197 --> 00:05:07,559
The answer is C.

111
00:05:07,559 --> 00:05:09,659
Fifty four square centimetres.

112
00:05:10,159 --> 00:05:13,607
Twelve point six zero has
four significant figures.

113
00:05:13,607 --> 00:05:15,826
Four point three has only two.

114
00:05:16,326 --> 00:05:19,139
The product is fifty four point one eight.

115
00:05:19,139 --> 00:05:20,770
Keep the fewest, two.

116
00:05:20,770 --> 00:05:21,893
So fifty four.

117
00:05:22,493 --> 00:05:23,956
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:23,956 --> 00:05:25,459
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:28,659 --> 00:05:31,495
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:31,895 --> 00:05:32,795
One.

121
00:05:32,462 --> 00:05:37,272
Why does two fifty point zero grams
say more than two fifty grams?

122
00:05:38,172 --> 00:05:39,072
Two.

123
00:05:38,754 --> 00:05:43,172
Why don't the zeros in zero
point zero zero two five count?

124
00:05:44,072 --> 00:05:44,972
Three.

125
00:05:44,697 --> 00:05:48,522
When adding measurements, what
decides the decimal places?

126
00:05:49,622 --> 00:05:53,918
Next time: the speed of light is
three with eight zeros after it.

127
00:05:53,918 --> 00:05:55,673
Which zeros were measured?

128
00:05:55,673 --> 00:05:57,782
Find out in scientific notation.
